<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Najaf</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Najaf is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, built around the shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>, the fourth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> and first Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>. It is one of the holiest cities of Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and for centuries has been a major centre of Shia learning, with its great seminaries and the vast Wadi al-Salam cemetery. It is the capital of Najaf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/najaf.jpg" alt="Najaf" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Goudarz.memar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shrine_of_Imam_Ali_Najaf_August_2023.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area lies about 30 km south of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. Excavations point to settlement from the 1st century BCE, and the area holds a large Christian burial ground with remains of the Sassanid period. Wadi al-Salam is said to have been a holy cemetery for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> called Baniqia. In Muslim tradition the city began with the burial of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>, whose grave was kept secret for fear of his enemies. The Abbasid Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> is said to have found it when a hunted deer took refuge at the spot, and he ordered a mausoleum, around which the town grew. The city's founding is dated to 791.</p><p>In the early 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited the shrine, then called Meshhed Ali, and described a city of rich merchants and a treasury filled with votive offerings. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took Najaf in the 16th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a> twice captured it during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-safavid-war-1623-1639">Ottoman–Safavid War</a> of 1623–1639 but lost it again in 1638. When the Portuguese traveller Pedro Texeira passed through in 1604 he found it in ruins with little more than 500 people, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> shifted eastward towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilla">Hilla</a>, leaving Najaf and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> dry.</p><p>In the 18th century the city's scholarly life came to be dominated by Farsi-speaking scholars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Water returned in 1803, after the Hindiyya Canal of 1793 and a century of Ottoman efforts to shift the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, and the last city wall was rebuilt in 1811. The Hawza of Najaf has been a centre of Shia education since the 11th century CE and was possibly established in the 9th; Murtadha al-Ansari and Ali al-Sistani studied there, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhollah-khomeini">Ayatollah Khomeini</a> lectured in the city from 1965 to 1978.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> were expelled in an uprising in 1915 and the British took over. In 1918 the sheikhs of Najaf rebelled and killed the British governor; the British besieged the city and cut off its water, and many Shia scholars were expelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, where they helped build up Qom. Najaf lost its religious primacy to Qom, which it did not regain until after 2003. From the 1950s many historic buildings near the shrine were demolished for new streets, and in 1958 the city wall was replaced by a ring road.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Najaf is the capital of Najaf Governorate, about 160 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, with a population estimated at about 1.4 million. The Imam Ali Mosque, with its gilded dome, is regarded by Shia Muslims as the third holiest Islamic site, and the city is a major pilgrimage destination. The nearby Wadi al-Salam is described as the largest cemetery in the world; many of the devout hope to be buried there in order to rise with Ali on Judgement Day.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Najaf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Although he pursued the idea through diplomacy and the religious conference at <b>Najaf</b> in 1743, the Ottomans never accepted it, the Shi'a clergy resented it, and it lapsed with his death.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Ghazi-ud-Din Haidar Shah instituted the Oudh Bequest, fixed payments by the British to the Shia holy cities of <b>Najaf</b> and Karbala, paid as interest on a loan Oudh made to the Company in 1825.</blockquote><a href="/empire/awadh-state">Awadh State · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Sunni and Shia Muslims share the core of their faith but differ in their understanding of leadership, in some laws and rituals, and in their sacred places: for Shia Muslims, the shrines of the imams at <b>Najaf</b>, Karbala and Mashhad are centres of pilgrimage.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Ruhollah Khomeini argued in lectures given at <b>Najaf</b> in 1970 that only a just jurist could ensure that government followed God’s law rather than the interests of kings or foreign powers.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>The family controlled the farming districts and the routes to the Shia shrine cities of <b>Najaf</b> and Karbala.</blockquote><a href="/state/mazyadid-dynasty">Mazyadid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>The early Musha'sha held extreme (ghulat) Shia beliefs: Muhammad's son Mawla Ali claimed that Ali ibn Abi Talib was divine and sacked <b>Najaf</b> in 1453.</blockquote><a href="/state/mushasha">Musha'sha</a></li></ul></section>
